          %h2#HurewiczTheorem Hurewicz theorem
          %p
            In general,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology+theory"} homology
            is a coarser invariant than
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            is the coarsest of all
            = succeed "-invariants." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+homology"} generalized homology
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HurewiczHomomorphism"}
          %p
            Indeed, the Hurewicz homomorphism exhibits a kind of abelianization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            (in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boardman+homomorphism"} Boardman homomorphism
            for more on this), a statement that in low degrees is true in the plain sense of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelianization"} abelianization
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HurewiczTheoremInDegreeZero"}
            and Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HurewiczTheoremInDegreeOne"}
            below.
          %p
            While in higher degrees the Hurewicz homomorphism is in general far from being an isomorphism, the thrust of the Hurewicz theorem is to show that high connectivity is a sufficient condition to ensure that it is. This is Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HurewiczTheoremInDegreeTwoAndHigher"}
            below.
          %p
          #HurewiczTheoremInDegreeZero.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (in degree 0)
              %br/
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a topological space, the Hurewicz homomorphism (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HurewiczHomomorphism"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+abelian+group"} free abelian group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[\pi_0(X)]
              on the set of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and the degree-0 singular homlogy:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{Z}[\pi_0(X)]
                    \\simeq
                    H_0(X)
                    \\,.
            %p
          %p
            Since a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            degree depends on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+component"} connected component
            of the base point, while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            does not depend on a basepoint, it is interesting to compare these groups only for the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
